  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1152 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nWindy & Warm Today:\n\nA nearly closed, well phased mid-upper level trough is quickly \nshifting northeast over the South/North Dakota and Minnesota border \nat forecast issuance on GOES mid-upper level water vapor imagery \nloops. The base of the trough over the Upper Mississippi River \nValley has increased winds from mid-upper level speed/directional \nconvergence with GOES-derived winds sampling a 80-100kt jet streak \nover the local forecast area. Accompanying tightening of the lower \nlevel geopotential height gradient also resulting in increased \nsurface winds overnight with VWPS at 925mb/850mb sampling 40 to \n50kts at KARX and KMPX. As the low center advects well north of the \nforecast area through today, surface winds veer, becoming out of the \nwest through the afternoon. Eventually the low exits northeast of \nthe region, allowing surface winds to relax tonight. \n\nWindy & Warm For Friday:\n\nThe reprieve in light winds is short lived as another Rocky Mountain \nLow lifts northeast in a similar trajectory through Friday, again \ntightening the low level geopotential height gradient, and \nincreasing south surface winds Friday into Friday night. Strongest \nwinds expected during the day Friday concurrent with peak daytime \nheating and diurnal mixing, with LREF member meteograms suggesting a \n2.5% (2/80 members GEFS+EPS) potential for 45+ mph gusts. Therefore, \nwhile winds expected to be strong, confidence for high wind criteria \nremains low at the current forecast hour.\n\nWeekend Precipitation Chances:\n\nAs the low shifts east through southern Canada on Saturday, an \nelongated frontal boundary shifts east across the Upper \nMississippi River Valley, increasing local precipitation \nchances. Current confidence for initial rainfall Saturday \nevening, increasing in coverage through the nighttime hours. \nSubsequent northern extent of ongoing rainfall into Sunday\nmorning remains a discrepancy between LREF members as most \nrecent GEFS(19.00Z)/EPS(18.18Z) continued their respective \ndecreasing/increasing trends for a narrow band of 0.5\" rainfall \naccumulation near the local forecast area. Location and eastern\nextent of frontal boundary through Saturday will ultimately \ndetermine focal point for increasing low level moisture flux\n(i.e., precipitation chances). Overall amounts and impacts \nappear minimal regardless.\n\nEarly Next Week: Cooler and Dry\n\nTemperatures remain on the cool side behind the cold front that \npasses through on Saturday as highs struggle to get out of the 30s \nand low temperatures stay in the upper teens to low 20s. Weak \nshortwave ridging moves overhead for early next week resulting in \ndry conditions. The next chance for precipitation to occur is \nmidweek when a shortwave trough moves into the Upper Midwest",
